A||||12||JohnnyK||Making my own aluminum bushings? What do you guys think?||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||09-23-2000||06:23 PM||||Since I need a new cradle bushing, I was told that I could machine one out of aluminum, and while I'm at it, do the other bushings too. What do you guys think of this? Aluminum too soft? Steel? It sounds like a great idea... I'll save tons of money!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||09-23-2000||08:26 PM||||Steel sounds like it would be awfully heavy, if you're thinking of duplicating the configuration of the OE rubber bushings.<P>Aluminum is lighter, and very easy to machine.  If you want to try it, go for it.  One of the Forum members has already tried it.  If you decide it's not for you, then you can bite the bullet and buy an OE one.  ||||reg||4
Z||000002||kevin||09-23-2000||08:47 PM||||That is correct, another Forum member has decided to put them out for general purchase ( I think). I believe it is 1fast2m6. This info is about 3 weeks old. Lets see if we can reserect him to see how his aluminum bushings are going. Of cource you can always go to Chris West who sells them for about $200. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Will||09-23-2000||11:12 PM||||I'll be doing just that sometime in the next few weeks.<BR>     I've looked at the OE cofiguration and it should be easy to duplicate. Most of the work can be done on a lathe, while a mill will be needed for two cuts on each of the rear bushings.<BR>     You should be able to make them from 4 inch bar stock, although they may be larger.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Rodrv6||09-24-2000||12:20 AM||||FST2M6 is the one who makes aluminum cradle bushings.  He installed a set on my car a couple of weeks ago.  I really like them.  No increase in road noise or vibration and the back end feels more solid.  I highly recommend them.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE||||reg||10
Z||000005||Philphine||09-24-2000||01:44 PM||||is it possible to get the demensioms for them? i may get the chance to try making some (shop may get a new lathe and mill) wat is that other synthetic material called (not the polyurethane, yhe harded plastic looking one)? we may have some around the shop.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||09-24-2000||01:51 PM||||Phil,<P>I believe you are thinking of DuPont Delrin nylon based product. Delrin should work fine for cradle bushings but it will likely need to be installed like pollyuerthane ones are.<P>One advantage to aluminum one is that you can make them to be pressed in just like the OE rubber ones. Then you don't have to fool with saving the sleve from the rubber ones.||||reg||1
Z||000007||JohnnyK||09-24-2000||04:51 PM||||But I want to try aluminum all around the car (a-arms, etc)... Is this ok, or will it wear away fast?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Will||09-24-2000||05:47 PM||||JohnnyK:<BR>     If you want hard suspension pivots, I would say use cartridge needle bearings pressed into an aluminum sleeve.<BR>     McMaster-Carr &lt;www.mcmaster.com&gt; should have the bearings you need. You will need to press them into a sleeve like the outer sleeve of the OE bushings, and you will need to machine an inner sleeve to serve the same purpose as the OE inner sleeve. The inner sleeve will be clamped in place by the pivot bolt, and the outer sleeve will be pressed intot he control arm. The bearings will be between them, providing an easy moving, non-binding suspension that does not deflect.||||reg||1
Z||000009||TOM||09-25-2000||12:07 AM||||I have full machining capabilities also. I am going to make bushings. Both cradle and suspension sets. When I can get some to measure. <BR>Also when/if you make the suspension bushings with the needle bearings you will need a way to lube them. Pretty simple but you need to think of it in advance. I thought some had a delrin sleeve?||||reg||1
Z||000010||JohnnyK||09-28-2000||11:11 PM||||After taking some measurments today, I think I'm going to do this (Talking about the front engine cradle bushings): There is no way I am going to be able to press the old bushings out, outer sleeve and all, so I'm going to make bushings that press into the old sleeve that I am going to leave there. My machine shop instructor says that will be fine. He thinks I should also just drill a hole through the aluminum for the bolt, but I think I should drill a slightly bigger hole, and push the old steel sleeve in there.. What do you guys think? ||||reg||1
Z||000011||lowCG||10-01-2000||12:03 AM||||You sholdn't need a sleeve with an all metal bushing.The plastic you want to use for bushings is UHMW,had it in the rear control arms for a year with no wear.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Will||10-01-2000||09:52 AM||||lowCG:<BR>     How were your UHMW bushings set up?<BR>     I'm going to be making XL UHMW bushings. I was thinking of machining the plastic to press into the OEM outer sleeves, and machining my own inner sleeves to be a slip fit in the plastic bishing.<BR>     "XL" means "cross linked". It refers to the plastic's moelcular structure. XL UHMW supposedly has better vibration damping characteristics than regular UHMW. It's rather expensive, however.<BR>||||reg||1
